': 'prepare_quiz:03ae1694-799b-45b5-7ddd-2c9314764e04', 'checkpoint_ns': 'prepare_quiz:03ae1694-799b-45b5-7ddd-2c9314764e04'}, 'parent_ids': ['25746ff2-05a9-4e7e-b796-440e14ad719d', '165f2b5e-3541-42dc-a805-a151ab6cffa5']}{'event': 'on_chain_stream', 'run_id': '48b0c192-e436-49ba-828a-b7087a732af6', 'name': 'prepare_quiz', 'tags': ['seq:step:1'], 'metadata': {'flow': 'quiz', 'thread_id': 'eeafa302-8c21-4c87-8975-f9753ecf5078', 'langgraph_step': 2, 'langgraph_node': 'prepare_quiz', 'langgraph_triggers': ('branch:to:prepare_quiz',), 'langgraph_path': ('__pregel_pull', 'prepare_quiz'), 'langgraph_checkpoint_ns': 'prepare_quiz:03ae1694-799b-45b5-7ddd-2c9314764e04', 'checkpoint_ns': 'prepare_quiz:03ae1694-799b-45b5-7ddd-2c9314764e04'}, 'data': {'chunk': {'question': 'Explanation: Multiplication is one of the four fundamental arithmetic operations, alongside addition, subtraction, and division. At its core, multiplying two numbers means adding one of them to itself as many times as indicated by the other. For example, 4 × 3 means “add 4 three times”: 4 + 4 + 4 = 12.\n\nKey concepts and properties:\n\n1. Repeated Addition:  \n   a × b = a + a + … + a (b times).  \n\n2. Commutative Property:  \n   a × b = b × a. You can swap the order of the factors without changing the product.\n\n3. Associative Property:  \n   (a × b) × c = a × (b × c). When multiplying three or more numbers, the grouping doesn’t affect the result.\n\n4. Distributive Property:  \n   a × (b + c) = (a × b) + (a × c). This allows you to break one factor into parts, multiply separately, then add.\n\n5. Multiplying Large Numbers:  \n   • Break one factor into place values (e.g., 76 = 70 + 6).  \n   • Use the distributive property to multiply in parts.  \n   • Sum the partial products to get the final answer.\n\n6. Zero and One Rules:  \n   • Any number multiplied by 1 remains the same.  \n   • Any number multiplied by 0 is 0.\n\n7. Practical Interpretations:  \n   • Arrays and area models: arranging objects in rows and columns corresponds directly to multiplication.  \n   • Scaling: multiplying by a factor increases or reduces quantities proportionally.\n\nUnderstan